8125, -0.02117919921875, 0.0066986083984375, 0.01291656494140625, -0.00681304931640625, 0.0219573974609375, 0.01708984375, -0.00949859619140625, -0.005161285400390625, 0.020416259765625, -0.0005278587341308594, 0.03155517578125, 0.00336456298828125, 0.0087738037109375, -0.00750732421875, 0.046417236328125, 0.0152435302734375, 0.01288604736328125, 0.0159912109375, -0.0208282470703125, -0.01544952392578125, 0.0111541748046875, -0.009796142578125, -0.0237884521484375, 0.03582763671875, 0.00978851318359375, 0.038848876953125, -0.0151519775390625, 0.016082763671875, -0.004119873046875, -0.006191253662109375, 0.01861572265625, -0.0078582763671875, 0.01593017578125, -0.0103607177734375, -0.020751953125, 0.033355712890625, -0.0291595458984375, -0.0191192626953125, 0.0120391845703125, -0.0015516281127929688, 0.01183319091796875, 0.00949859619140625, 0.053680419921875, 0.0391845703125, -0.0092315673828125, -0.0137939453125, 0.004589080810546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: The aim of this study was to develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance, with the goal of supporting standardized reporting in clinical practice to reduce errors and improve patient care.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. The multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three sequential agents: (1) a structuring agent that segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using regex rules and local large language models; (2) a consistency-checking agent that detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections or within sections; and (3) a QA agent that identified gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented communication of critical findings using rule-based and language model prompts. Each agent operated independently, passing its output to the next agent in sequence. This modular approach, without automated feedback loops or error correction between agents, was chosen to facilitate error tracing and allow for independent evaluation of each agent\u2019s performance.\n\nFor radiologist evaluation, a random subset of 45 reports was selected using stratified random sampling to represent the distribution of body regions (chest, abdomen, pelvis) and types of flagged issues (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, critical findings) present in the full dataset. Stratification ensured the sample reflected the proportions of each body region and flagged issue type observed in the overall dataset as closely as possible given the sample size. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed these reports. \u201cCorrectly restructured\u201d was defined as accurate anatomical section assignment for the entire report without altering original content; \u201cincorrectly restructured\u201d indicated misassignment or loss of information at the report level; \u201cdisagreement\u201d reflected differing reviewer assessments per report. No consensus process was used to capture independent reviewer perspectives and avoid potential bias, though this may limit reliability.\n\nInter-rater agreement was calculated using Cohen\u2019s kappa, with interpretation based on the Landis and Koch scale (values between 0.61 and 0.80 are considered substantial agreement).\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content. The system flagged 90 of 638 reports (14.1%, 95% CI: 11.5\u201317.0%) for at least one issue, including section mismatches (80 of 638 reports, 12.5%, 95% CI: 10.1\u201315.4%), gender-anatomy conflicts (5 of 638 reports, 0.8%), and undocumented communication of critical findings (3 of 638 reports, 0.5%).\n\nSummary of radiologist evaluation (n = 45 reports):\n\n| Outcome                  | Number (%)         | 95% CI         |\n|--------------------------|-------------------|----------------|\n| Correctly restructured   | 31 (69%)          | 53\u201382%         |\n| Incorrectly restructured | 2 (4%)            | 0\u201313%          |\n| Disagreement             | 12 (27%)          | 15\u201342%         |\n\nCohen\u2019s kappa for agreement was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement per the Landis and Koch scale. Both reviewers independently assessed whether any clinically important information was omitted or any fabricated content was introduced; both agreed that neither occurred. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" in 24 of 45 reports (53%), \"good\" in 14 of 45 reports (31%), and \"fair\" in 7 of 45 reports (16%), corresponding to 38 of 45 reports (84%, 95% CI: 70\u201393%) rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\".\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system successfully combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow, demonstrating favorable performance in radiologist evaluation. These results suggest that such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice, with the potential to streamline workflows, improve clinical communication, and reduce errors. Future work will focus on expanding the system to additional report types, incorporating automated feedback mechanisms between agents, and further improving error detection.\n\nLimitations:\n- Retrospective design, which may introduce selection or information bias\n- Single-institution setting\n- Limited sample size for radiologist evaluation\n- Potential sources of error such as misclassification of anatomical sections or missed inconsistencies\n- Reviewer selection may introduce bias, as only board-certified radiologists from the same institution participated; future studies should include external reviewers to improve generalizability\n- Generalizability to other institutions, imaging modalities, or report types remains to be established; multi-center validation is recommended\n\nCompliance: The study was conducted with institutional review board approval and in compliance with data privacy regulations; all data were anonymized and stored on secure, access-controlled servers.\n\nReference:\nLandis JR, Koch GG. The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data. Biometrics. 1977;33(1):159-174."}}